 # cmux Core Control
+
+## External caller policy (local Atrium adaptation)
+
+Upstream: manaflow-ai/cmux v0.65.2.2, commit 12023d89feb5425c766ba05e8d8e4cfc176e3991. This policy overrides the caller/focus defaults and illustrative unscoped commands below and in linked references. Read both `cmux` and `cmux-workspace` before external terminal work.
+
+- Resolve targets from explicit user authorization or this task's creation results. Missing targets mean ask the user or create a new window; environment IDs and the visually focused context are not authorization. Metadata listings may inform a user choice, never silently select a target.
+- Pass an explicit absolute `--socket` on every call; do not auto-discover another instance. Keep socket, window, workspace, and surface identities together. Supply `--workspace` and `--surface` for input and screen reads, and `--window` for workspace creation. Revalidate UUIDs after restart or a missing target; do not substitute another target.
+- Create only in newly created or explicitly authorized containers. Use `--focus false` where supported. If addressing fails, stop and report it rather than changing focus to recover. Do not reuse or tidy old helper panes.
+- Text, Enter, Ctrl+C, and creation with `--command` are side effects. Unknown outcomes or timeouts require read-only inspection and, if unresolved, user confirmation, not automatic resending. Only read-only queries may be retried within a bound. `--command` already includes one Enter. CLI success is not shell completion; read real output.
+- Let the official CLI read its official local password store. Keep credentials out of skills, prompts, command arguments, logs, and controlled-shell environments; do not export `CMUX_SOCKET_PASSWORD` or print full environments. Presence-only credential checks are sufficient.
+- cmux and the user own terminal/task lifetime. Atrium exit, agent stop, idle recycling, and archive do not close or stop external tasks. There is no archive-time all-tasks-stopped guarantee. These are skill conventions, not a same-user Bash permission sandbox or exactly-once delivery.
 
